"Textbook: OpenStax <em>Astronomy 2e</em>, §{#} to §{#} (introducing general relativity, spacetime and gravity, tests of general relativity). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/astronomy-2e. The physics behind it: OpenStax <em>University Physics</em>, Vol. {#}, §{#} (Einstein’s theory of gravity). Access for free at https://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-{#}.",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Earth or rocket?; Rocket at {#} g; Falling is floating; A very wide room: tides (these use the Windowless room view); Light in a rocket at g and Light at {#}¹⁵ g (the Light in the box view); {#} eclipse: Einstein, {#} eclipse: Newton and Radio telescopes today (the {#} eclipse view); Mercury’s perihelion and Relativity alone (the Mercury view). This worksheet uses <strong>Rocket at {#} g</strong>, <strong>Falling is floating</strong>, <strong>Light in a rocket at g</strong>, <strong>Light at {#}¹⁵ g</strong>, <strong>{#} eclipse: Einstein</strong> and <strong>Mercury’s perihelion</strong>.",
"The Windowless room view shows two closed rooms side by side, Room A and Room B. One stands on the ground on Earth; the other is a rocket far from any planet. In each, a person stands on a scale and holds a ball; Play lets go of the balls. Below, a graph shows each ball’s height against time.",
