"Test Einstein’s equivalence principle in a windowless room, then follow it to two predictions about gravity, the bending of light and the turning of Mercury’s orbit, and check them against the {#} eclipse and a century of watching Mercury.",
"<strong>What a scale reads.</strong> A bathroom scale measures how hard the floor pushes up on you. Standing still on Earth, the floor holds you up against gravity, and the push is your weight \\(mg\\), with \\(g = 9.81\\) m/s². In an elevator speeding up on its way up, the floor must push harder, \\(m(g + a)\\), so you read heavier. In a rocket far out in space, with no gravity at all, the floor must push you along with the rocket: if it speeds up at a, the scale reads \\(ma\\). A scale calibrated on Earth turns the push into “kilograms” by dividing by g.",
"<strong>The happiest thought.</strong> In {#} Einstein realized that a person falling off a roof would feel no weight. Inside a closed room you cannot tell standing still on Earth from speeding up at g in a rocket: a dropped ball falls to the floor in the same time, \\(t = \\sqrt{2h/a}\\) for a drop of height h, and the scale reads the same. That is the <strong>equivalence principle</strong>: locally, gravity and acceleration cannot be told apart. Falling freely is the same as floating with no gravity, which is why astronauts in orbit float: they and their station fall around the Earth together.",
"<strong>Gravity bends light.</strong> A beam of light enters one wall of an accelerating rocket and crosses to the other. From outside it goes straight, but while it crosses, the floor speeds up toward it, so it lands a little lower than it started:",
"\\[\\text{drop} = \\tfrac12 a\\left(\\frac{L}{c}\\right)^2\\]",
"for a room of width L. If a rocket bends light, then by the equivalence principle gravity must bend light too. Near the Sun, Einstein’s theory predicts that starlight grazing the Sun’s edge bends by {#} arcseconds (″), twice what Newton’s gravity would give if light were a stream of particles. Two expeditions measured it at the total eclipse of {#} May {#}, at Sobral (Brazil) and Príncipe (off West Africa).",
"<strong>Mercury.</strong> Mercury’s elliptical orbit slowly turns, so its closest point to the Sun (the perihelion) moves around the Sun. The pulls of the other planets explain most of the turning; about {#}″ a century was left over. Einstein’s theory adds exactly that amount, with nothing adjusted to fit. A full turn is {#}° × {#}″/° = {#}″.",
"\\[c = 3.00\\times10^8\\ \\text{m/s},\\quad 1\\ \\text{kg} \\leftrightarrow 2.205\\ \\text{lb on an Earth scale},\\quad \\text{proton diameter} \\approx 1.68\\times10^{-15}\\ \\text{m}.\\]",
